 "response": "Gedebren's budget is now **$2,224K**.\n\n**How I got there**\n\n1. I kept only Finance Office records that change the budget: one set, increases, decreases and withdrawals. Messages from PMO, Engineering, Sales, Vendor, Legal, Marketing, Facilities and Operations were ignored. So were non-budget Finance items such as invoices, receipts, room moves and audits.\n2. I worked out which withdrawals still count, following each chain from the last withdrawal back:\n   - #0009 withdraws #0005. #0039 withdraws #0009, and #0474 withdraws #0039. So #0039 is cancelled, #0009 stands, and #0005 is withdrawn.\n   - #0024 withdraws #0006. #0043 withdraws #0024, #0063 withdraws #0043, and #0667 withdraws #0063. So #0043 stands, #0024 is cancelled, and #0006 still applies.\n   - #0054 withdraws #0032. Its chain runs #0206 \u2192 #0219 \u2192 #0224 \u2192 #0947 \u2192 #1399, and works out so that #0054 is cancelled. #0032 still applies.\n   - Other chains resolved the same way include those for #0015, #0013, #0089, #0091, #0184, #0225, #0356 and #0400.\n3. I started at $1,710K (#0001). I then applied every update that was not withdrawn, in record order. The running total ends at $2,224K after the last record, #1600 (+$60K).\n\nThis total depends on many hand-checked steps across 1,600 records, so a single slip in a chain or a sum would change it.\n\nANSWER: $2224K",
